{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MosaicIonsToElectrolytes",
    "description": "MOSAIC's liquid-phase electrolyte formation, SUBROUTINE ions_to_electrolytes(jliquid) of chem/module_mosaic_therm.F (WRF v4.8.0), with calculate_xt, form_caso4 and form_camsa2. It turns the ions a liquid phase holds into the electrolytes MOSAIC's activity model (compute_activities, mtem_activities.esm) and ZSR water (aerosol_water_zsr.esm) are written over, and is the first thing MESA's mesa_flux_salt does on every iteration.\n\nINPUT: the liquid species amounts a_in (`sp` order so4, no3, cl, nh4, msa, na, ca; nmol per m3 of air), clipped at zero.\n\nCALCIUM SULFATE first: min(Ca, SO4) forms CaSO4, which is insoluble, so the routine moves it straight to the solid (caso4 is reported for the consumer to add there).\n\nTHE DOMAIN is decided on X_T of what is left (template aerosol_xt): X_T >= 1.9999 or X_T < 0 is sulfate-POOR (the Fortran's own split here is 1.9999, not the 2 of compute_activities and mesa_estimate_eleliquid).\n\nSULFATE-POOR: the H+ needed for neutrality is the anion excess max(cat_net, 0), and every cation is paired with every anion in proportion to their charge equivalents (template ion_pair_electrolyte): E = (x_c n_a M_a + x_a n_c M_c)/M_E. No HSO4- exists here, so NH4HSO4, NaHSO4 and H2SO4 come out zero, as the Fortran sets them.\n\nSULFATE-RICH: Ca(MSA)2 forms first, then the remaining MSA takes Na and NH4 in proportion to their amounts (or, if MSA is in excess, all of them, the rest being free MSA acid). The cations left over, x_s per sulfate, apportion the sulfate: x_s <= 1 acid + bisulfate, 1 < x_s <= 1.5 bisulfate + letovicite, above that letovicite + neutral sulfate, each salt split between ammonium and sodium as f_nh4 : f_na. Nitrate and chloride are HNO3 and HCl. Without sulfate the Fortran leaves after the MSA salts (HNO3 and HCl stay zero).\n\nWritten as the closed-form apportionment (s_acid, s_bisulf, s_lvc, s_sulf) instead of the Fortran's three branches; each product keeps the Fortran's association ((x f) SO4), and a zero fraction gives the exact zero the Fortran leaves.\n\nPRECISION: double-precision arithmetic; the literal 1.9999 is binary32 (mosaic_mesa_parameters.esm), 2., 0.5, 3., 1.5 are exact.\n\nTESTS: the liquid at the start of a mesa_ptc iteration and the electrolytes mesa_flux_salt formed from it, from the MESA tape (WRF fork module_esm_mesa_tape, kernels/mosaic_therm_driver; data/eqwefic/chem170_scm/mesa64): column hand-offs of the chem170 capture replayed bit for bit, and hand-specified bins for the branches the column never reaches. Real64 at rel 1e-9. Mutations caught: the 1.9999 split moved to 2 (2), letovicite/sulfate split at 1.4 (1), no HNO3/HCl in the sulfate-rich domain (2; the hand bin keeps them through driver mode 5, since conform_electrolytes degasses them). Not reached by any pass: a CaSO4 move (calcium has already left as CaSO4 when a pass starts) and a negative liquid amount, so the clip and the CaSO4 transfer are untested.",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R.A., Easter, R.C., Fast, J.D., Peters, L.K. (2008). Model for Simulating Aerosol Interactions and Chemistry (MOSAIC). J. Geophys. Res. 113, D13204."
      },
      {
        "citation": "WRF v4.8.0 chem/module_mosaic_therm.F"
      }
    ]
  },
